version: "2.0"
name: gnomon-kernel-broker
description: >-
  The approval broker: approval cards and their classes, presence-bound
  decisions, standing grants, strictness levels, batch cards, and the special
  cards (install, theft, daemon stop, unlock, API token, arm custody, run mode).
context:
  - >-
    broker.ts and card.ts raise and decide cards; standing-grant.ts,
    strictness.ts and batch-card.ts shape what a card asks and how long an
    answer stands.
  - >-
    Split out of packages/gnomon/.purpose (2026-10-27); path: values stay
    relative to packages/gnomon, the package root.
components:
  batch-card:
    description: >-
      #batch-card (founder 2026-10-23: "grouped cards of related things, one
      password"). src/kernel/broker/batch-card.ts batchCardBlock: a batch
      card's lines wrapped under MAX_LINE (never cut) and measured after
      wrapping. Shared by #rules-apply and #mcp-servers so both batch cards
      follow ONE set of semantics: validate every item before any write or
      card; over the caller's cap refuse, never cut; at allow re-check each
      item and skip, and report, any that changed while the card waited.
      The GUI scrolls a long body inside the card (design-system
      card.body_scroll). Tests: test/mcp-servers.test.ts (#batch-card),
      test/rules-view.test.ts.
    path: src/kernel/broker/batch-card.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, broker, cards, paradigm-8]
    oracles: [G-MCP-07]
  standing-grant:
    description: >-
      TD-2026-10-20-086 F6 (spec §3.4 "Standing grant"). The grant card is
      raised with RaiseInput.standingGrant: its allow is FRESH class (one
      presence, no root signature; Aegis open point 1); other grant cards stay root.
      src/kernel/broker/standing-grant.ts. ONE root-class `grant` card
      (request.grant.standing{tuples, repos} from the human on the GUI only,
      detail.op 'standing') mints grant.standing{grantId (= the cardId),
      cardId, tuples, repos, device (the GUI device's udev: unlock key),
      expiresAt ≤ 12 h, by}; the same touch keeps the device's cached unlock
      so later uses can be signed by the helper. While it stands, a human
      GUI decision on the same device that a tuple covers
      (STANDING_TUPLES: mode.loosen:auto-edits, permission:always,
      voice.widen, voice-arm:allow, daemon-stop:restart with
      detail.binaryUnchanged and no update; NOT reconsider:extend, Aegis M2; repo-scoped
      ones only with a non-null granted repo) takes no prompt: approval.decide
      {presence:'standing:<grantId>'} then the notify row
      grant.standing.used{grantId, cardId, tuple, decision, repo, hsid}.
      Never standing: root class, hand-only, install, gate-class,
      fallback-cloud, declassify, peer-pair, api-token, grant, handoff,
      loop-trust (8.0:loops), peer-flagged or irreversible cards (standingTupleOf
      + broker checks).
      Ends: expiresAt, Revoke (request.grant.standing.revoke{grantId} →
      grant.standing.revoked{grantId, by, reason:'human'}, a tighten, no
      presence), the next screen lock / sleep / user switch or a helper exit
      (broker.helperSessionEnded/helperExited → endAll). StandingGrantBook
      folds kernel-signed rows only (forged rows ignored), like RuleBook /
      RunModeBook; status.standing{built, tuples, active[], pendingCardId}.
      No new signal (events are the §9.1 grant.standing* kinds).
    path: src/kernel/broker/standing-grant.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, broker, approvals, presence, td-2026-10-20-086]
    tests:
      - test/standing-grant.test.ts
  approval-broker:
    description: >-
      8.0:loops (gnomon-loops.md §3.1, §3.3): classes.ts rows loop-trust
      {allow root, deny fresh} (never always, never standing, hand-only) and
      loop-choice {continue fresh hand-only, stop cached}; log-append.ts adds the
      human request.loop.* kinds to the first-party request gate. Test:
      test/loop-classes.test.ts.
      Aegis M1: a root-capable card's detail carries rootDecisions (hashed).
      TD-2026-10-20-514 follow-up: a client decisionSig takes the root-only
      decision.sign form (Aegis F6, 1 prompt); a root-capable card's block
      carries {type:'root-rows', rootDecisions, detail} for the GUI (C1e);
      helperBatch()/refreshHelperBatch() = status.helper.batch (Q2);
      RaiseInput.rootDecisions raises voice.enrol allow to root (Q3).
      TD-2026-10-20-514: every root-class card carries detail.rootRows; #approve
      prepares the rows (RaiseInput.prepare), builds the gnomon-root-decision:v2
      row (rowsDigest) and makes ONE fresh prompt with signDecisionBatch on a
      helper >= 1.8.0, else the legacy one-signature-per-row path over the same
      prepare/commit; the effect gets rowSigs; prepare released in a finally.
      STANDING GRANT (TD-2026-10-20-086 F6): StandingSeam (setStanding) is
      asked before any presence; a match is signed under the device unlock
      (or a verified client decisionSig) with presence 'standing:<id>' and
      #standing-grant writes grant.standing.used after approval.decide.
      RUN MODES: approval.auto modeRef is mode:auto-edits (file edits) or
      mode:auto-edits:read-only / mode:ask:read-only (a Bash command
      #shell-readonly proves read-only; TD-2026-10-20-294 part 1, F1
      TD-2026-10-20-086; path = the anchored root's realpath, inputHash the
      request's) or rule:<cardId>. Not built (would move clientApiId, founder
      gate): approval.auto.miss and an anchoredInputHash field.
      Slice C6'a2-4: auto() also takes author ('human' only: a brokered "/"
      run) and callId (call:<ULID>, the call's tool.brokered row's too).
      PREFIX SCOPE (TD-2026-10-20-294 part 3): approve{decision:'always',
      scope} — {kind:'exact'} is the v1 exact rule; {kind:'prefix', prefix}
      (1-3 words) is accepted only on a Bash permission card whose SHOWN
      command parses via simpleArgv as one literal simple command whose argv
      starts with exactly those words (else BAD_REQUEST); decisionSig is the v2
      layout whose last line is scopeDigest(scope) (signer decisionMessage
      {scopeDigest}), the presence binding carries scopeDigest, the prompt
      names the prefix, approval.decide records scope and
      approval.rule.learned records {match:'prefix', prefix}. replay.ts
      verifies a scoped always ONLY over the scope layout. A Bash card whose
      command is one literal simple command carries detail.prefixOptions (first
      1-2 argv words) + a {type:'bash-prefix', detail} block node the GUI
      verifies against contentHash.
      #approval-broker (spec §3.4, §5 verb 8, §3.1 approvals key; §19.1 M1.6).
      W4 phase A (Windows hybrid, TD-2026-10-22-278): an ApprovalsKey with
      clickClass (platform/win32/approvals.ts: the win32 helper's Software-KSP key,
      bound to the human by a root-signed gnomon-approvals-key:v1 row; dev homes
      via `gnomon dev up`) signs a CACHED-class GUI decision of a CLICK_KINDS card
      (permission, ask-user, plan) with no presence: approval.decide{presence:
      'click'}, no unlock. Everything else on such a key refuses presence-
      unavailable kernel-side (cached, batch), and fresh only with W4 phase B's
      SEPARATE Windows Hello presence key bound (presencePublicKeyPem, a
      root-signed gnomon-presence-key:v1 row; `gnomon dev up` creates it with one
      Hello prompt): the approvals key signs the decision, the Hello key the
      presence challenge (verified against presenceKeyId, never the click key);
      no unlock is ever kept. A click never learns a rule (rules.ts takes
      fresh/cli only); click-beyond-class is a suspicion. The GUI's approve calls
      wait 150 s (a PIN prompt), above the kernel's 120 s helper sign timeout.
      Tests: test/identity-hardware.test.ts, test/win32-approvals.test.ts.
      Voice (TD-2026-10-19-194): whether voice may take a decision is
      gnomon-contracts' VOICE_ALLOWLIST (#contract-voice-allowlist, §14.7), not
      classes.ts; test/voice-golden.test.ts pins abiId and checks the list
      against KIND_DECISIONS.
      src/kernel/broker/. Helper 1.6.0 (V5, TD-2026-10-19-194, §14.7, S29-K5):
      arm-custody.ts VoiceArmCustody keeps voice-arm REFERENCES only (the
      unlock is in the helper per armId) and appends approval.voice.disarm
      {armId, reason, cause?} for every arm on a helper session push (reason
      per cause from contracts VOICE_DISARM_REASONS: screen-lock (also
      screensaver), display-sleep, system-sleep, user-switch), helper exit
      (helper-exit) or a pair connection closing (conn-closed). A push also drops every cached-unlock
      reference (helperSessionEnded); connectionClosed sends conn.close.
      HelperApprovalsKey gains openArm, signUnderArm, closeArm,
      closeAllArms, connectionClosed and onHelperPush; HelperProcess parses
      the v2 push (a malformed one kills the helper); MIN_HELPER_VERSION
      1.6.0, HELPER_PROTOCOL gnomon-helper:v2 (test/voice-arm-custody.test.ts,
      test/identity-helper-pin.test.ts). T-2026-10-18-028: M2 — UnlockStore ends a cached
      unlock at UNLOCK_IDLE_MS idle, UNLOCK_ABSOLUTE_MS (60 min) after it
      opened, or after UNLOCK_MAX_SIGNATURES (64) uses (approval.unlock.
      dropped{reason: idle|expired|exhausted}); M3 — unlock.drop goes to the
      helper on every close and failure path (connectionClosed always, a
      lost keep-sign reply, a close during the keep-sign, a failed cached
      sign, an ended unlock), and HelperApprovalsKey.dropUnlock escalates a
      drop the helper does not answer to unlock.drop-all, then recycles the
      helper process (HelperTransport.running / recycle); helper floor
      MIN_HELPER_VERSION 1.3.0 (test/unlock-bounds.test.ts).
      M4 — the presence prompt (drawn by the OS, not a conformant client)
      gets the card line through promptSafe (every Cf, Cc, Zl, Zp removed)
      and the summary QUOTED with quote/backslash escaped (quoteSummary,
      card.ts); the helper strips the same set from the caller text
      (Protocol.swift promptSafe).
      M5 (#session-runner, platform/darwin/session-sbpl.ts, profile v4): every
      session profile ends with helperDenySbpl() — deny process-exec of
      /GnomonHelper.app(/|$) and /gnomon-helper$, deny file-read* of the
      bundle (no copy out), regexes on resolved paths; backstop: the helper
      refuses serve/once under any sandbox (test/session-helper-deny.test.ts).
      L2 — #freshPrompt: at most ONE outstanding fresh presence prompt per
      connection (FORBIDDEN{presence, why: prompt-outstanding}), and
      PROMPT_COOLDOWN_MS (3 s) after a prompt the human declined
      (why: prompt-cooldown, retryAfterMs); an accepted prompt or an
      unavailable helper starts no cooldown; PromptRateLimited passes the
      presence error mapping unchanged (test/prompt-rate.test.ts). P8-D96: RaiseInput.decisions limits a card's signed
      actions (permission slice cards: allow|deny, no always); approve
      refuses any other decision bad-params; expire(cardId, why) logs
      approval.expire{into:'deny', why}; noteOf; suspect (approval.suspect
      from kernel observation). Aegis A3: approve claims the decision
      synchronously after its last re-check (CardRecord.claim); expire()
      refuses a claimed card and defers while #deciding (applied in
      approve's finally if the decision did not settle). classes.ts (P8-D98: the reserved daemon-stop kind
      is accept|reject, both fresh presence — Touch ID, TD-2026-10-19-528 F3) — valid decisions per card kind and the
      BASE presence class of each (cached: allow/deny on permission, answer,
      approve-plan, reject on plan/reconsider/inbound handoff, deny on
      strictness, incident; fresh: everything else the spec names, incl.
      always; root: install, use-api-token, peer-pair, grant; none: withdraw);
      irreversible and peer flags raise a cached decision to fresh; a decision
      the spec does not class is fresh (fail closed). card.ts — the card is
      composed from the broker's own record: lines sanitized (hidden-chars /
      bidi → visible escapes + card flags, §4.5), contentHash over {kind,
      requestedBy, hsid, detail, flags}, actions[] kernel-signed per valid
      decision (domain gnomon:card-action:v1), and the presence prompt composed
      from the card record (kind, requester, contentHash summary, connId, open
      GUI sessions {n}, the class reason) and signed by the kernel key (domain
      gnomon:presence-prompt:v1); verifyPresencePrompt checks text = the
      record's composition. Line cap MAX_LINE (512 code points, exported for
      #manifest-loader's install-card wrapping); the presence summary takes
      the first line's first 120 code points, never splitting a surrogate
      pair. install-card.ts passes the install card's pre-sanitized lines
      together with their sanitizer flags. unlock.ts — the cached unlock
      (TD-2026-10-20-086 F4/F5, superseding S25-H1 and the M2 60 min / 64):
      created only by a fresh presence on a GUI connection, never for a CLI
      connection; KEYED BY THE GUI DEVICE (broker unlockKeyOf: `udev:` + 40
      hex of sha256 over the launch-token deviceId; a gui context without a
      deviceId keeps its connId) so it survives a reconnect and is shared by
      the device's GUI sessions; ends at screen lock / sleep / user switch
      (helper push), helper exit, 12 h (UNLOCK_ABSOLUTE_MS = UNLOCK_IDLE_MS)
      or 512 signatures, each bound LOWERED to the helper's: helper 1.7.0
      reports `bounds` on a kept sign (helperBoundsOf), 1.6.0 reports none and
      is held to LEGACY_HELPER_BOUNDS (15 min idle, 60 min, 64). Dropped rows
      name the last connection and scope:'device'
      (test/unlock-device.test.ts). broker.ts — ApprovalBroker: raise (approval.request before
      the card exists; permission cards time out into deny, fallback and
      api-token never expire), approve (human only, B4; voice FORBIDDEN gate
      voice `voice-off` for every form, G-ABI-02 voice row; via link refused
      `peer-via`; held ⇒ withdraw only; peer-flagged always refused
      `peer-always`; decisionSig by the approvals key over approveMessage —
      bad ⇒ AUTH bad-signature, no approvals key ⇒ UNSUPPORTED presence
      M1.3; presence per class with a PresenceToken BOUND to {cardId,
      contentHash, decision} on the kernel-trusted clock (T-2026-10-18-003);
      always under a cached unlock ⇒ FORBIDDEN presence when no fresh presence
      comes; root class signs the decision with the root key; approval.decide
      {decisionSig, presence, via, connId, cli provenance} BEFORE the effect;
      always ⇒ approval.rule.learned; approval.suspect{why, provenance} for
      unknown CLI provenance / no TTY / CLI pgid inside a live session /
      unlock beyond class), expireDue, decisionOf (silence and unknown cards
      ⇒ deny, B6), connectionClosed, status. Production presence is
      PRESENCE_UNAVAILABLE, the approvals key NO_APPROVALS_KEY and the root
      unprovisioned until M1.3: every presence-taking decision refuses.
      install-card.ts — the M1.5 install-card decision moved under the broker
      (T-2026-10-18-008): the card is rendered from the detail RE-DERIVED from
      the pinned snapshot (manifest/install.ts rederiveInstallDetail) and kept
      in kernel memory; presence is a mod.install token bound to {cardId,
      contentHash} handed unburned to decideInstall. api-token.ts — the per-use
      billing api-token card (human-only request, root+fresh use-api-token,
      never expires), keystore injection into exactly one child env
      (takeEnvFor, single use; KEYSTORE_UNAVAILABLE until M1.3),
      billing.api.card.requested / active (activation and every turn) /
      ended / refused and the status indicator API CREDITS ACTIVE. port.ts —
      the #socket port: approve, permit.reserve (+ release), connection close,
      status cards / budget / billing, guiSessions[].unlock. The spec declares
      no signals; events are §9.1 approval.* / billing.* kinds. Round 2
      (Aegis C1): GUI vs CLI is decided by the authenticated credential (only
      a launch-token GUI session may hold an unlock), never by the hello
      surface; expiry skips a card being decided and the decision re-checks
      the card and the connection after its last await (one terminal row);
      the sanitizer strips tag characters and escapes C0/C1 controls; the
      install card is rendered by the broker from re-derived lines, readPaths
      and linked source validated, presence bound to the decision; effect
      failures are recorded as exactly one approval.effect.failed{cardId,
      decision, effect: the card kind, reason: a kebab-case code} and no
      approval.suspect row; the card stays pending (approval.decide is a
      record, never authority; P8-D98 slice A2), so replay folds decide ->
      approval.effect.failed -> decide for one card with no divergence (the
      last decide stands). raise refuses a card carrying both a ruleScope and
      an effect BAD_REQUEST (Aegis M3: a learned rule auto-allows without
      running an effect). decisionSig v2 (P8-D98
      slice A2): an `answer` signs the v2 message (signer decisionMessage,
      first line gnomon:decision:v2, last line answerDigest = the noteHash)
      and its presence binding carries answerDigest. With a client-supplied
      decisionSig an answer rewritten on the wire after signing fails AUTH
      bad-signature; on the helper-signs path the kernel computes
      answerDigest from the note it received, so v2 binds the text the
      kernel received, not the text the human saw (Aegis M2); an answer with no note is
      refused BAD_REQUEST; every other decision stays v1. Replay verifies
      both layouts given decisionKeys (replay.ts checkDecisionSigs) with a
      per-log cutover MARKER ROW: the first v2 answer or any 8.0:P8-D98 kind;
      a v1 answer after it is decision-sig-v1-after-cutover. The helper
      prompt line (gnomon-helper Protocol.swift helperLine) parses the v2
      marker so it still shows the decision, card and content hash, plus the
      answer digest. Slice 2: with no
      approvals key (before M1.3) approve answers UNSUPPORTED{presence}
      before the decisionSig shape check, so the GUI (no signing path yet)
      sees the presence refusal (T-2026-10-18-018 item 2); status.cards per
      F4 — the human gets pendingForYou + byKind, every other class counts
      only {n, pendingForYou: 0, held}; theft-card.ts — a logged
      socket.auth.theft (#socket dispatch → port.authTheft) raises ONE
      fresh-presence gate-class card per revoked connId naming it, no
      timeout, no effect (the revocation stands either way). Round 2
      (Aegis C2): the card's promise is stored before the raise awaits
      (concurrent theft rows for one connId raise one card), at most
      MAX_OPEN_THEFT_CARDS (8) theft cards are open — past that a theft
      coalesces into the newest open card — a failed raise is forgotten so
      the next theft row retries, and #socket raises only after the
      socket.auth.theft row was written. The path #web-endpoint (revokes,
      reports theft) → #socket (writes the row) → #approval-broker (raises
      the card) is a one-row relay with no state of its own, documented
      here and in theft-card.ts rather than as a $flow node (a new spec
      node moves Appendix A's count; see lore).
      M1.3 slice 1: with a helper-held approvals key (#identity
      HelperApprovalsKey) an `approve` WITHOUT decisionSig has the helper
      make it: a cached-class decision on a GUI connection with a live
      unlock signs `cached` (no prompt; a helper that lost the unlock ⇒
      approval.unlock.dropped{helper-lost} and a fresh touch); otherwise one
      fresh touch signs the decision and the bound presence challenge,
      keeping the unlock in the helper for cached-class GUI decisions only.
      Unlock drops (idle, closed) propagate to the helper; a helper exit
      drops every unlock (approval.unlock.dropped{helper-exit}). A helper
      that is down or returns a signature that does not verify ⇒
      UNSUPPORTED / FORBIDDEN presence, card stays pending. Root-class
      decisions use the root's signRowAsync. A client-supplied decisionSig
      is still verified as before. No new wire reason or event kind.
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 5 (Orca audit Adopt 5): deny-safe settlement
      pinned by falsifiers, no code change — an undecodable request is
      denied (permission-malformed ⇒ deny line; bad approve params ⇒
      BAD_REQUEST, card stays pending; answerFor: only a decoded allow
      allows); a cancel (control_cancel_request ⇒ expire 'cancelled')
      settles into deny once and answers the session nothing; a late answer
      on a non-pending card is refused before presence with no
      approval.decide row; an unknown card is NOT_FOUND. Tests:
      test/broker-deny-safe.test.ts (every platform).
      F3 (TD-2026-10-20-086): RunModeControl configDefault = the
      root-signed config `runModes.default` (budget/config.ts
      parseRunModesSection; any fallback ⇒ null): a repo with no mode of its
      own takes it (view source 'config') when its folder is known and not
      home / above home / `/` (else ask; a repo key with no live session ⇒
      ask, fail safe); the ceiling still applies; status.runModes
      .configDefault (test/run-mode-default.test.ts).
      RUN MODES (P8-D86/D87, src/kernel/broker/run-mode.ts + supervisor/
      auto-edits.ts): manual | ask (default) | auto-edits, per repo and per
      session, folded from KERNEL-signed approval.mode.set and
      session.open{runMode} rows (RunModeBook, catchUp over modeSourceOf; a
      repo's mode persists across sessions and restarts). A session takes its
      repo's mode unless set for it. Effective = stricter(chosen, ceiling);
      ceiling L0/L1 auto-edits, L2 ask, L3 manual; strictness levels are NOT
      built (no level source ⇒ ceiling auto-edits; status.runModes.strictness
      .built false). RunModeControl.request serves log.append request.mode.set
      from a human on a gui or device credential only (dispatch.ts
      `requests`): BAD_REQUEST on an unknown mode/keys, NOT_FOUND on a dead
      hsid, FORBIDDEN{gate strictness, strictness-mode} over the ceiling;
      writes request.mode.set, then tightening ⇒ approval.mode.set{scope,
      from, to, by, presence:'none'} at once; loosening ⇒ a strictness card
      (allow fresh/hand-only, deny cached; decisions allow|deny so never
      always; 2 min ⇒ deny) whose effect re-checks the ceiling and writes
      approval.mode.set{presence:'fresh'}; one pending loosening card per
      scope. session.open{runMode}: no looser than the repo ⇒ set by the open
      row; looser ⇒ opened at the repo's mode + a loosening card
      (result.runModeCard). Supervisor: manual denies Bash/PowerShell/Edit/
      Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit, WebFetch and mcp__* (manualHolds) with no
      card until a plan card in that session is approved (reset whenever
      manual begins; RunModeControl onChange → supervisor.noteModeChange
      records every applied change on each live session at once, Aegis L2);
      auto-edits passes Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit whose path is
      absolute with no `..` (M4), under a root that is not the user's home,
      above it or / (M3, isHomeOrAbove; also refused at admitOpen and
      request.mode.set), with NO symlink component below the root (every
      component lstat'd, the final one too; H1), not gate-class (layers.ts
      gateClassPathRule, incl. editor/toolchain dirs, lint/format configs,
      task/build files, other agents' steering files, non-ASCII and win32
      alias names; H2; TD-2026-10-20-294 re-check C2: .gitconfig,
      .git-credentials, .netrc, curl/pip/conda config, interpreter and
      toolchain pins, build entry points, shell rc files, .env/.env.*) and whose card has no hidden/bidi flags, after
      broker.auto ⇒ approval.auto{hsid, tool, path (real), inputHash,
      modeRef:'mode:auto-edits'} (a failed row falls through to the card);
      everything else cards as in ask. Tightening a REPO pulls every live
      session whose own mode is looser down to it with its own
      approval.mode.set and withdraws a pending loosening card for it (M1).
      permission.ts deniedWrite also tests policy.ts realpathFollow (where
      a write lands, dangling links followed) and denies an unresolvable
      path (H1).
    path: src/kernel/broker/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, broker, approvals, presence, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    tests:
      - test/approval-broker.test.ts
      - test/run-modes.test.ts
      - test/broker-deny-safe.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/manifest-loader.test.ts
      - test/identity-hardware.test.ts
    oracles: [G-BRK-01, G-BRK-02, G-BRK-03, G-BRK-05, G-ABI-02, G-SUB-02, G-SUB-03]
  strictness-levels:
    description: >-
      #strictness-levels (spec §3.4, D86–D88; M2.11-lite, build plan step 9),
      a sub-component of #approval-broker in src/kernel/broker/strictness.ts.
      Per-repo levels L0–L3 folded from KERNEL-signed
      approval.strictness.raised|lowered rows only (StrictnessBook; an unknown
      level folds as L3; no row is L0, the D87 L1 floor waits on Move 3).
      request.strictness.set{repo, to} (repo: a key or an absolute path) from the human on a gui or
      device connection only (dispatch.ts requests.strictness; every other
      class FORBIDDEN{matrix}, G-STR-01). Tightening writes the request row and
      approval.strictness.raised{presence:'none'} at once, withdraws a pending
      loosening card and runs the repo brake (the loops pause). Loosening is one
      level per step through a strictness card (detail.op level.loosen, allow
      fresh presence, hand-only, no standing tuple; G-STR-04); its effect
      re-checks the level and writes approval.strictness.lowered{presence:
      'fresh'}. The level is run-mode.ts's `level` seam (the L0/L1 auto-edits,
      L2 ask, L3 manual ceiling) and the loops' repo cap; status.strictness.
      Not in the lite slice: signal score, hysteresis, evidence, the L1
      floor, batched L2 landing, the cached-unlock remap.
    path: src/kernel/broker/strictness.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, approvals, strictness, paradigm-8]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/strictness-levels.test.ts
    oracles: [G-STR-01, G-STR-04]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/broker/strictness.ts
      - src/kernel/broker/run-mode.ts
      - src/kernel/socket/dispatch.ts
      - src/kernel/lifecycle/daemon.ts
